                <pb facs="0179" n="85" sameAs="frus:frus1887/pg85"/>

                <head>No. 70.<lb/><hi rendition="#italic">Mr. Hall
                        to Mr. Bayard</hi>.</head>
                <opener><dateline rendition="#float-right"><placeName><hi rendition="#small-caps"
                                >United States Legation in Central America</hi>,<lb/><hi
                                rendition="#italic">Guatemala</hi></placeName>, <date
                            calendar="gregorian" when="1886-12-18"><hi rendition="#italic"
                                >December</hi> 18, 1886</date>. (Received January 4,
                        1887.)</dateline>
                    <seg rendition="#float-left">No. 598.]</seg></opener>
                <p><hi rendition="#small-caps">Sir</hi>: I had the honor to inform you by my
                    dispatch No. 589, of the 3d instant, of the arrival in this city on the 29th
                    ultimo of the commissioners of Nicaragua and Costa Rica, appointed in accordance
                    with the invitation of the Government of Guatemala to agree upon the bases of a
                    settlement of their dispute concerning their boundary, I have now to report that
                    up to this time no progress whatever has been made towards a settlement.</p>
                <p rendition="#center">* * * * * * *</p>
                <p>By the next mail I may be able to communicate more definite information. * *
                    *</p>
                <p>I have, etc.,</p>
                <closer><signed><hi rendition="#small-caps">Henry C. Hall.</hi></signed></closer>
            </div>
        </body>
    </text>
